```html
<article class="essay essay--clarity">
  <header class="essay__header">
    <h1 class="essay__title">Clarity Doesn’t Come First</h1>
    <p class="essay__subtitle"><em>Why action often precedes understanding</em></p>
    <p class="essay__deck">Clarity is often treated like a prerequisite. In practice, it is more often earned in motion.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="essay__content">
    <p>We are often taught to treat clarity like a prerequisite: as if we must understand the whole path before taking the first step. But real life rarely works that way. More often, clarity arrives in motion. We begin, we stumble, we notice, we adjust. The path does not reveal itself all at once; it yields gradually, in response to our willingness to walk.</p>

    <p>That is the difference between <strong>total clarity</strong> and <strong>usable clarity</strong>.</p>

    <p>Total clarity is a fantasy of completeness. It promises certainty before action, resolution before risk, and a guarantee that the choice we make will remain correct in every future version of ourselves. But life does not usually offer that kind of assurance. Waiting for it can become a disguised form of fear: the postponement of living until the moment when living feels safe.</p>

    <p>Usable clarity is humbler, and more honest. It does not tell you everything. It tells you enough. Enough to begin. Enough to act in good faith. Enough to take the next honest step without demanding a full map of the territory ahead.</p>

    <p>This changes how we understand mistakes. A wrong turn is no longer a verdict on our competence or worth. It becomes information. A correction. A kind of feedback from reality. Experience is not what happens after you know what you’re doing — it is how you discover what you’re doing.</p>

    <p>That is why the phrase <strong>“I move, and then I learn”</strong> feels so important. It carries humility because it admits uncertainty. It carries trust because it refuses paralysis. It carries intelligence because it treats movement as a source of knowledge. And it carries resilience because it allows revision without shame.</p>

    <p>Many of the most meaningful things in life are not permanent at first. They begin as experiments: a job, a relationship, a practice, a new way of being. Some are only for a season. Some are scaffolding. Some are invitations that lead somewhere unexpected. Not every path needs to be forever to matter deeply now.</p>

    <p>Maybe this is the deeper courage: not the absence of fear, but the willingness to let reality outgrow your first idea of it. To begin before certainty arrives. To stay teachable. To trust that the next step, while incomplete, can still be enough.</p>

    <p>And often, it is.</p>
  </div>

  <aside class="essay__pullquote">
    <blockquote>
      <p>Clarity arrives in motion.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </aside>

  <footer class="essay__footer">
    <p class="essay__bio">Your Name writes about uncertainty, attention, and the psychology of growth.</p>
  </footer>
</article>
```

